{"meta": {"course": "cat100-supplement", "chapter": "02", "section": "07", "chapter_title": "Resume, Branding, and AI Brainstorming", "title": "Peer Review for Professional Materials", "description": "CAT 100 textbook section connecting peer review for professional materials to peer review feedback log, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the peer review feedback log shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."], "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_resume_branding_and_ai_brainstorming", "peer_review", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 7, "top_tier_hardened": false, "prereq_section_ids": ["cat100-supplement/02/06"], "practice_ids": ["cat100_supplement_2_7_peer_review_for_professional_materials_artifact_signal", "cat100_supplement_2_7_peer_review_for_professional_materials_annotation_signal", "cat100_supplement_2_7_peer_review_for_professional_materials_ai_boundary", "cat100_supplement_2_7_peer_review_for_professional_materials_calibration"]}, "content": "# Peer Review for Professional Materials\n\n![Peer Review for Professional Materials textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/02-07-peer-review-for-professional-materials.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\n\"Looks good!\" is the most useless thing you can say to a classmate who asked you to review their resume. It names no location, no problem, and no fix, so the writer leaves with exactly what they came with. This section teaches how to give peer feedback that the writer can actually use, and, just as important, how to do it without violating the writer's privacy. Reviewing professional materials is different from reviewing an essay, because resumes carry phone numbers, addresses, and employment history, and \"let me run this through an AI to check it\" can expose a peer's personal data to a system neither of you controls.\n\nThe skill matters on both ends. Good feedback you give makes you a better self-reviewer of your own work, because you internalize what specific, actionable comments look like. And handling a peer's document responsibly, keeping their data off public tools and leaving the final call to them, is a digital-citizenship habit you will need any time you handle someone else's information at work.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the peer review feedback log shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nNina swaps resumes with a classmate for peer review. To save time, she pastes his entire resume, name, phone number, home address, and all, into a free AI tool and asks it to \"list everything wrong.\" The tool returns useful phrasing notes, but Nina has just handed a stranger's personal contact information to a system whose data practices she never read, for a person who never consented to it. The phrasing feedback was fine; the privacy breach was not, and it was entirely avoidable, she could have stripped the contact block first or reviewed it herself.\n\nThe case shows the two responsibilities that ride together in peer review: making the feedback useful and keeping the other person's data safe. Strong feedback delivered through a careless data move is still a failure, because the cost lands on someone who trusted you with their document.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nUseful peer feedback follows a simple shape: **location, problem, question.** Name where the issue is (\"Experience, bullet two\"), name what is wrong with it (\"does not say what you did or the scale\"), and pose it as a question that hands the decision back to the writer (\"what was your actual task, and how many events?\"). The question framing matters because the document belongs to the writer; your job is to surface issues, not to seize the pen.\n\nTwo boundaries govern professional-materials review specifically:\n\n| Boundary | What it requires | Why it matters |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Ownership | Comment and question; do not rewrite for them | The writer must be able to defend every claim |\n| Privacy | Strip personal data before any tool use; do not share it | A peer's contact info and history are theirs, not yours to expose |\n\nThe hardest feedback to give well is on a risky claim, a line like \"Led the team\" that the writer may not be able to defend. The move is not to correct it silently but to raise it as a question (\"were you the formal lead, or one contributor?\"), because only the writer knows the truth and only the writer will be in the interview. Polishing a peer's wording while leaving an indefensible claim, or exposing their data to fix a comma, both miss the point.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Your peer asks you to \u00e2\u20ac\u02dcrun my whole resume through an AI and tell me what is wrong.\u00e2\u20ac\u2122 Their resume includes their full name, phone, and home address. What is the right move?\" explanation=\"The phrasing feedback may be useful, but pasting a peer\u00e2\u20ac\u2122s contact block into a tool you cannot audit exposes data that is theirs, not yours. The fix is cheap: strip the personal details first, or review it yourself. The privacy boundary holds even when the feedback would be good.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Strip the name, phone, and address first (or review it yourself), then check phrasing\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Paste the whole thing in, the feedback is what matters\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Rewrite their resume for them so they do not have to\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Tell them it \u00e2\u20ac\u02dclooks good\u00e2\u20ac\u2122 to avoid the issue\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example: Turning a Vague Reaction Into Usable Peer Feedback\n\nYou are reviewing a classmate's resume bullet:\n\n```\nHelped with events for the student government.\n```\n\n**Step 1. Write the unhelpful comment first, so you can see what to avoid.**\n\n```\nLooks good!\n```\n\nThis gives the writer nothing. It names no location and no fix.\n\n**Step 2. Rewrite it as specific, actionable feedback using \"location + problem + question.\"**\n\n```\nExperience, bullet 2: \"Helped with events\" does not say what you did or the\nscale. What was your actual task, and how many events or attendees? A recruiter\nwill want a verb and a number here.\n```\n\n**Step 3. Raise the risky claim as a question, not a correction.** Suppose another line reads \"Led the team.\" You do not rewrite it; you ask:\n\n```\nWere you the formal lead, or one contributor? If a recruiter asks in an\ninterview, which answer can you defend?\n```\n\n**Step 4. Respect the data boundary.** You do NOT paste the peer's full resume, with their phone number and address, into a public AI tool. If you use a tool to check phrasing, you remove the personal details first.\n\n**Step 5. Hand back a decision-ready set.** The writer ends with three located comments they can accept or decline, ownership intact, and their private data never exposed.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Turn a reaction into feedback the writer can act on\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"A classmate\u00e2\u20ac\u2122s bullet reads \u00e2\u20ac\u02dcHelped with events for the student government.\u00e2\u20ac\u2122 Your first instinct is to write \u00e2\u20ac\u02dcLooks good!\u00e2\u20ac\u2122 Why is that comment worthless to them?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"It is too short to fit the margin\",\"It names no location, no problem, and no fix, so the writer leaves with what they came with\",\"It uses the wrong tone\",\"It mentions the student government\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Right. \u00e2\u20ac\u02dcLooks good\u00e2\u20ac\u2122 performs reviewing without improving anything. The writer cannot act on praise that points to nothing.\"},{\"prompt\":\"You rewrite it as \u00e2\u20ac\u02dcExperience, bullet 2: helped with events does not say what you did or the scale.\u00e2\u20ac\u2122 What turns this from a correction into usable peer feedback?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Adding a stronger verb yourself\",\"Ending with a question only they can answer: what was your task, and how many events?\",\"Rewriting the whole bullet for them\",\"Marking it with a red flag\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Yes. Location + problem + question hands the decision back to the writer. You surface the issue; they keep the pen.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Another line reads \u00e2\u20ac\u02dcLed the team.\u00e2\u20ac\u2122 You do NOT rewrite it. You raise it as a question: \u00e2\u20ac\u02dcWere you the formal lead, or one contributor? If a recruiter asks, which answer can you defend?\u00e2\u20ac\u2122 Only the writer knows the truth.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Before you use any AI tool to polish phrasing on their document, what must you do first?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Ask for their grade\",\"Remove their name, phone, address, and employer details, or review it yourself\",\"Add your own name as co-author\",\"Make the font match yours\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Correct. A resume carries exactly the data that fuels identity theft. Strip it before any tool touches it, or do the review yourself.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nThe same review, reduced to its order: be specific and located first, then protect the writer\u00e2\u20ac\u2122s ownership and their data, then hand the decision back.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Name the location, the problem, and a question or specific fix (not \u00e2\u20ac\u02dclooks good\u00e2\u20ac\u2122)\",\"Raise any risky claim as a question, not a silent correction\",\"Strip personal data (name, phone, address, employer) before any tool use, or review it yourself\",\"Hand back comments the writer can accept or decline, ownership intact\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore you hand back peer feedback, check both the usefulness and the privacy of how you produced it.\n\n1. **Make each comment actionable:** does it name a location, a problem, and a question or specific fix? \"Looks good\" and \"fix this\" both fail.\n2. **Raise risky claims as questions, not corrections:** when a line may not be defensible, ask whether the writer can back it, rather than rewriting it for them.\n3. **Protect their data:** if you use any tool to check phrasing, remove the name, phone, address, and employer details first, or review it yourself.\n4. **Leave the decision with the owner:** end with comments they can accept or decline, not a rewritten document they must adopt.\n\nFeedback that is specific but exposes a peer's data fails the rule, and so does feedback that protects their data but says nothing useful. Both must hold.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nOne misreading is that helpful means nice. Vague praise feels kind but leaves the writer worse off than honest, located comments. The kindest review is the one the writer can actually act on.\n\nA second misreading is that the privacy boundary is paranoia. It is not abstract: a resume carries exactly the data (full name, phone, address, work history) that fuels identity theft and unwanted contact, and pasting it into a free tool can store it on servers you cannot audit. Treating a peer's contact block as sensitive is the normal professional standard, not an overreaction.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nReview one weak bullet end to end. Your classmate wrote \"Helped with events for the student government.\" First, write the unhelpful version on purpose so you can see what to avoid: \"Looks good!\" names nothing. Now apply location, problem, question: \"Experience, bullet two: 'helped with events' does not say what you did or the scale. What was your actual task, and how many events or attendees? A recruiter will want a verb and a number here.\" That comment points to a spot, diagnoses it, and hands the writer a question only they can answer.\n\nNext, suppose another line reads \"Led the team.\" Do not rewrite it; raise it: \"Were you the formal lead, or one contributor? If a recruiter asks, which answer can you defend?\" Then handle the tool boundary: if you want to check phrasing with an AI assistant, you remove the classmate's name, phone, and address first, because their personal data is not yours to upload. You finish by handing back three located comments the writer can accept or decline, ownership intact and private data never exposed. The walkthrough shows the two responsibilities working together: the feedback is usable and the data stayed safe.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader notices the difference between a comment that improves the document and a comment that just performs reviewing. \"Strong action verbs!\" performs; \"bullet two needs a number\" improves. Reading this section well sharpens your ear for the difference in your own comments.\n\nA strong reader also treats the privacy boundary as a default, not a special case. Before any document touches a tool, they ask \"is there personal data here, and whose is it?\" Building that reflex, rather than remembering it only when prompted, is what separates digital citizenship from a checklist.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nThe location-problem-question shape and the data boundary transfer to every review you will ever do of someone else's work. Editing a friend's grad-school essay, commenting on a coworker's draft proposal, or reviewing a teammate's slide deck all reward specific, located, question-framed feedback that leaves ownership with the author. And any document holding someone else's personal information, a colleague's contract, a customer's form, demands the same data restraint you practiced on a peer's resume. The content changes; the dual obligation to be useful and to be careful with others' data does not.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"peer_review\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the peer review feedback log packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/02-07-peer-review-for-professional-materials-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"peer review feedback log\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"02.07\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the peer review feedback log.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the peer review feedback log for CAT 100 02.07. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_peer_review_for_professional_materials\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links claim, evidence, tool boundary, revision, and remaining limitation. That is what separates digital fluency from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_2_7_peer_review_for_professional_materials_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "peer_review", "concept_ids": ["peer_review", "cat100_supplement_resume_branding_and_ai_brainstorming"], "stem": "Your classmate hands you their resume for peer review. Which comment is the most useful professional feedback?", "options": ["\"Looks great, nice job!\"", "\"I'd change some things but it's basically fine.\"", "\"Your second bullet says 'helped with events' but does not say what you did or how many; can you name a task and a number?\"", "\"I don't really like the font.\""], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "Useful peer feedback points to a specific location, names the problem, and suggests a concrete fix. Praise and vague reactions give the writer nothing to act on.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_2_7_praise_is_review", "difficulty": "easy"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_2_7_peer_review_for_professional_materials_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "peer_review", "concept_ids": ["peer_review", "evidence_based_revision"], "stem": "You are reviewing a peer's cover letter and you spot a claim you think is exaggerated. What is the most professional way to raise it?", "options": ["Rewrite the whole paragraph yourself the way you would say it.", "Mark it 'wrong' and move on.", "Ask a question: \"This says you 'led the team'; were you the formal lead, or one contributor? A recruiter may ask.\"", "Ignore it, since pointing out problems is rude."], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "Good peer review raises concerns as questions that keep ownership with the writer, rather than rewriting their work for them or staying silent about a real risk.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_2_7_rewriting_for_them", "difficulty": "medium"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_2_7_peer_review_for_professional_materials_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ai_supported_learning", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning", "peer_review"], "stem": "A peer reviewer pastes your whole resume into a public AI chatbot to \"get suggestions.\" Your resume includes your home address and phone number. What is the concern?", "options": ["No concern; AI tools are private by default.", "Sharing another person's contact details with a third-party tool without permission is a privacy problem; identifying data should be removed first.", "The only concern is whether the suggestions are good.", "It is fine as long as the peer deletes the chat afterward."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Peer review carries a duty of care with someone else's data. Pasting a peer's personal contact information into a third-party tool without consent is a privacy violation; strip identifying details first.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_2_7_sharing_peer_data", "difficulty": "hard"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_2_7_peer_review_for_professional_materials_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "peer_review", "concept_ids": ["peer_review", "evidence_based_revision"], "stem": "You received three peer comments. Which response shows you are using review well, rather than just collecting it?", "options": ["Apply every comment exactly as written so no reviewer feels ignored.", "Apply none of them because they are only opinions.", "Accept the two that match the job posting, decline the third with a one-line reason, and note all three decisions in your revision log.", "Thank the reviewers and submit the original unchanged."], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "Using feedback means judging each comment against your goal, acting on the ones that fit, and recording why you declined the rest. Blind acceptance and blanket rejection both skip the judgment.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_2_7_apply_all_comments", "difficulty": "medium"}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "a081782391a27c6cca1a8f49f7856de091deb55f17bec7e2ff1bda567f0ffc91", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_2_7_praise_is_review", "pattern": "praise_counts_as_review", "description": "Believing that positive, general comments (\"looks great\") count as a completed peer review", "trigger": "reviewer leaves only praise or vague reactions with no specific, actionable note", "feedback": "Useful review names a location, the problem, and a concrete fix. \"Looks great\" gives the writer nothing to act on.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_2_7_rewriting_for_them", "pattern": "rewriting_instead_of_advising", "description": "Taking over a peer's document by rewriting passages instead of flagging issues for them to decide on", "trigger": "reviewer replaces the writer's wording rather than raising a question or suggestion", "feedback": "Keep ownership with the writer. Raise concerns as questions or suggestions, not as a takeover of their voice.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_2_7_sharing_peer_data", "pattern": "sharing_peer_data_without_consent", "description": "Pasting a peer's resume, including personal contact details, into a third-party tool without permission", "trigger": "reviewer submits another person's identifying data to an AI tool during review", "feedback": "Peer review carries a duty of care. Strip identifying details before using any third-party tool, and get consent.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_2_7_apply_all_comments", "pattern": "applying_all_feedback_uncritically", "description": "Treating every peer comment as a required edit instead of judging each against the goal", "trigger": "writer applies all comments verbatim or rejects all of them without reasons", "feedback": "Judge each comment against your goal. Act on the ones that fit, decline the rest with a reason, and log the decisions.", "rail_action": "practice"}]}